Z||000003||SLYGUY||09-20-2001||01:59 PM||||I don't think so. I thought the 88 with the V6 came with the Getrag.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000004||c m west||09-20-2001||03:26 PM||||Well, we have found that the bracketry mounting the slave cylinder on the Isuzo transmissions will become bent over time and will cause the piston inside the slave to travel through the bore at an awkward angle, thereby allowing air into the system, with only partial amounts of fluid escaping<P><BR> But you may still have air in the system - try cracking the line at the slave, having someone press the clutch pedal down, then close the fitting once pure fluid and no air is coming out - continue as neccessary||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||09-20-2001||03:36 PM||||Much of the problem probably lies with the parts themselves.  I used to buy the aftermarket stuff, because I thought they were just as good, but now I have learned my lesson (I have seen the light, so to speak).<P>All aftermarket slaves are made by the same manufacturer and sold under the various brands (Raybestos, Wagner, etc.) All are cast iron and are inherently flawed.  Some people say they have used them with no issues, but the reality is they are in the minority.  <P>I can't say that all aftermarket master cylinders are the same, but I would be leary of these as well.  A few years ago I replaced the clutch in an '88 Formula.  At the same time I replaced the slave and master cylinders (both from NAPA).  Within a 6 month period I re-replaced the slave 4 times and the master twice!  I finally go fed up and bought a real GM slave and real GM master.......no more problems.<P>I know it is too late for you at this point, but I would return the master and exchange it for another one.  If that still doesn't help then I would ask for my $$ back and buy a GM part.<P>Another bonus with using the GM stuff is you can fully bleed the system in just a few tries.  The aftermarket stuff never seems to fully bleed.....which is the main issue I had with them.<P>BTW......the caps sometimes are hard to get off.  This is especially true if it has been years since the cap was last removed.  Use a pair of channel locks or something equivelant to unscrew the cap while holding the master in place (so you don't twist it), or just put more muscle into it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>    ||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||09-20-2001||03:54 PM||||I agree with Mrfiero because like him I thought the after market parts were OK. Those after market slave cylinders are junk. My clutch would not fully disengage no matter how many times I bleed it. I finally bought a GM one and have had no problems since. It even bleeds easily!<P>They even look completely different from each other. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||recluse||09-20-2001||04:08 PM||||I agree.  I've rebuilt clutch masters and slaves and bought after market parts and Fiero store parts but the clutch never felt 100% right.  I just replaced both with GM parts a few days ago and this is the best the clutch has ever felt. I did notice it was easier to bleed too.  Now i wonder why i wasted all that money on aftermarket junk!||||reg||14
Z||000008||aubicon||09-20-2001||09:53 PM||||thanks for all the advise. does anyone know where the best place to get the GM master and slave cylinders. the dealerships around here are kind of expensive. and about the cap on the original master cylinder. yes mine was very hard to get off. i used two pairs of channel locks one on the cap and one on the reservior. on the bottom of the reservoir there is a little loop or hole in the plastic, if you thred a zip tie through that and around the body of the master cylinder and synch it down it makes the reservoir mutch more solid which helps when removing the cap.||||reg||1
Z||000009||qwikgta||09-22-2001||02:20 AM||||SLYGUY...be careful, I broke off my res when I tryed to take off the top.  After I got a new one i put a black Ziptie around the plastic base and tyed it off to the power brake booster bracket.  Now it has some support that will not let it be twisted the way the lid twists off.<P>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000010||SLYGUY||09-22-2001||02:45 AM||||Thanks for the tip quikgta...<P>I was fearful of putting any muscle into removing the cap and ending up with a busted reservoir. <P>I'll use that tip to hold it down while I twist.<P>I have an idea that it is low in fluid or needs to be bled because I have to push the pedal all the way to the floor to disengage the clutch and even with new fluid in the tranny and pedal to the floor it is still notchy shiftin'<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||09-22-2001||04:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SLYGUY:<BR><B><BR>I have an idea that it is low in fluid or needs to be bled because I have to push the pedal all the way to the floor to disengage the clutch and even with new fluid in the tranny and pedal to the floor it is still notchy shiftin'<P>Sly</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>If it is engaging right off of the floor then it is definitley a hydraulic issue.  Bleeding the clutch would be the first step.  You likely have a bad slave and/or master too, but obviously bleed it first to see what happens.<P>BTW......most Getrag's (especially those with miles on them) tend to be a little notchy.  Don't expect that to change much once the fluid issue is worked out.  It will get better, but it will likely remain somewhat notchy.<BR> <BR>Good luck. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||William Federle||09-22-2001||05:05 PM||||I bought a new clutch master cylinder from Advance Auto Parts for my 1984 SE about 2 years ago for $109.00 . The only trouble I had was attaching it to the new steel clutch pedal that I got at the same time from the fiero store. I had to fabricate an adaptor bushing to make it fit with no slop. Lucky for me the slave was OK.<P>It's all still working. Knock on wood.||||reg||1
